**1. Double-render bug — FOUND and FIXED, in `## Load State Handling`**

Before:
```kotlin
Box(modifier = Modifier.fillMaxSize()) {
    when (articles.loadState.refresh) {
        is LoadState.Loading -> { CircularProgressIndicator(...) }
        is LoadState.Error -> { ... }
        is LoadState.NotLoading -> {
            if (articles.itemCount == 0) EmptyState(...)
            else ArticleLazyColumn(articles = articles)
        }
    }
    PullToRefreshBox(
        isRefreshing = articles.loadState.refresh is LoadState.Loading,
        onRefresh = { articles.refresh() }
    ) {
        ArticleLazyColumn(articles = articles)   // <-- second, sibling render
    }
}
```
The `when` block and `PullToRefreshBox` were siblings inside the same `Box`. Once loaded, `NotLoading` rendered `ArticleLazyColumn`, and `PullToRefreshBox`'s own content lambda rendered `ArticleLazyColumn` again — the list was drawn twice, stacked on top of itself in the `Box` (and during a load, `CircularProgressIndicator` and `PullToRefreshBox`'s own indicator would show simultaneously). This matches the "same item/loading state renders twice" bug described in the audit.

Fix: made `PullToRefreshBox` the single container and moved the `when` state handling inside it, so exactly one call path to `ArticleLazyColumn` exists at a time. Added an inline comment explaining why (see the "Fixed (audit)" callout and code block now in the file, right above `## Load State Handling`'s code sample).

